{"action_id":"a0004","additional_user_input":[],"audience":"unspecified","constraints":["Do not propose computational checks; return an empty proposed_checks list."],"context":null,"goal":null,"inputs":{"branches":{"a":{"answer":"There are 7 such subsets. Partition them by their unique largest element. The cases are:\n\n- largest 4: {2,3,4}\n- largest 5: {1,3,5}, {4,5}\n- largest 6: {1,2,6}, {3,6}\n- largest 7: {2,7}\n- largest 8: {1,8}\n\nThus the count is 1+2+2+1+1=7. For example, {1,8} qualifies. Every nonempty target-sum subset has one unique largest element; the displayed remainder enumerations in each largest-element case are complete, so the cases are disjoint and exhaustive.","approaches":[{"description":"Partition each subset by its largest element and enumerate the subsets of smaller elements that supply the remaining sum.","id":"max-element-partition","outcome":"candidate","reason":"The largest element is unique, giving disjoint cases; the small residual sums permit direct complete enumeration."}],"change_log":[],"claims":[{"basis":"question_premise","basis_reference":"Question premise: \"How many subsets of {1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8} have sum 9?\"","citations":[],"critical":false,"depends_on":[],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"target-definition","kind":"definition","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"The objects considered are subsets of {1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8} whose element-sum is 9.","step_ids":[],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Derived by the largest-element case enumeration in steps partition-by-maximum through count-cases.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":["target-definition"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"complete-case-list","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"The target-sum subsets are exactly {2,3,4}, {1,3,5}, {4,5}, {1,2,6}, {3,6}, {2,7}, and {1,8}.","step_ids":["partition-by-maximum","enumerate-remainders"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"The seven listed sets are distinct and exhaustive by steps partition-by-maximum, enumerate-remainders, and count-cases.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":["complete-case-list"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"subset-count","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"Exactly 7 subsets have sum 9.","step_ids":["count-cases"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"It occurs in the exhaustive list established in enumerate-remainders.","citations":[],"critical":false,"depends_on":["complete-case-list"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"qualifying-example","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"{1,8} is a qualifying subset, since 1+8=9.","step_ids":["enumerate-remainders"],"tool_ids":[]}],"open_questions":[],"proof_steps":[{"citations":[],"depends_on":[],"id":"partition-by-maximum","justification":"A subset summing to 9 is nonempty and has a unique largest element m. Since all elements lie in {1,...,8}, m is in that set; subsets with m at most 3 have sum at most 1+2+3=6, so only m=4,5,6,7,8 can occur. Distinct values of m define disjoint cases.","statement":"Partition all target-sum subsets according to their largest element m in {4,5,6,7,8}."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["partition-by-maximum"],"id":"enumerate-remainders","justification":"For m=4, the remainder 5 from {1,2,3} is only {2,3}. For m=5, the remainder 4 from {1,2,3,4} is {4} or {1,3}. For m=6, the remainder 3 is {3} or {1,2}. For m=7, the remainder 2 is {2}; for m=8, the remainder 1 is {1}. Each statement follows by listing the distinct-element subsets of the indicated set whose sum is the indicated residual value.","statement":"The five largest-element cases yield respectively 1, 2, 2, 1, and 1 subsets."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["partition-by-maximum","enumerate-remainders"],"id":"count-cases","justification":"The cases are disjoint by their unique largest elements and exhaustive by partition-by-maximum. Their sizes total 1+2+2+1+1=7.","statement":"The exhaustive disjoint case count is 7."}],"question_status":"answered","tool_requests":[]},"b":{"answer":"There are 7 subsets. One example is {1,8}. Exhaustively: no singleton sums to 9; the four pairs are {1,8}, {2,7}, {3,6}, {4,5}; the three triples are {1,2,6}, {1,3,5}, {2,3,4}; and any subset with at least four positive distinct elements has sum at least 1+2+3+4=10. Thus 4+3=7.","approaches":[{"description":"Classify subsets by cardinality, use the minimum possible sum for four distinct positive elements to eliminate all larger cardinalities, then list pairs and triples systematically.","id":"cardinality-casework","outcome":"candidate","reason":"It gives a finite disjoint partition of every possible subset and explicitly accounts for each case."},{"description":"Directly inspect all 256 subsets.","id":"brute-force-enumeration","outcome":"rejected","reason":"It is unnecessary for a self-contained exhaustive proof and would not explain the structural cutoff at four elements."}],"change_log":[],"claims":[{"basis":"question_premise","basis_reference":"Question premise: \"subsets of {1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8} have sum 9\".","citations":[],"critical":false,"depends_on":[],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"given-set-target","kind":"assumption","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"The objects under consideration are subsets of {1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8} whose element-sum is 9.","step_ids":[],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Derived in step-cardinality-bound.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":["given-set-target"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"at-most-three-elements","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"Any qualifying subset has at most three elements.","step_ids":["step-cardinality-bound"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Derived in step-pairs and step-triples.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":["at-most-three-elements"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"seven-total","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"Exactly seven subsets have sum 9.","step_ids":["step-pairs","step-triples","step-total"],"tool_ids":[]}],"open_questions":[],"proof_steps":[{"citations":[],"depends_on":[],"id":"step-cardinality-bound","justification":"Among four distinct positive integers, the least possible sum is 1+2+3+4=10. Every member of the given set is positive, so a subset of four or more elements cannot sum to 9.","statement":"A qualifying subset has at most three elements."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["step-cardinality-bound"],"id":"step-singletons","justification":"Every available element is at most 8, so no singleton has sum 9; the empty subset has sum 0.","statement":"There are no qualifying subsets of size zero or one."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["step-cardinality-bound"],"id":"step-pairs","justification":"For a pair {a,b} with a<b and a+b=9, a can only be 1, 2, 3, or 4; these give respectively b=8,7,6,5. Hence the complete pair list is {1,8}, {2,7}, {3,6}, {4,5}.","statement":"There are exactly four qualifying pairs."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["step-cardinality-bound"],"id":"step-triples","justification":"Write a<b<c and a+b+c=9. If a=1, then b+c=8 and b is 2 or 3, giving {1,2,6} and {1,3,5}. If a=2, then b+c=7 and b=3, giving {2,3,4}. If a>=3, then a+b+c>=3+4+5=12. Thus these three triples are complete.","statement":"There are exactly three qualifying triples."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["step-singletons","step-pairs","step-triples"],"id":"step-total","justification":"The cases by subset cardinality are disjoint. Sizes zero and one contribute 0, size two contributes 4, size three contributes 3, and larger sizes are excluded by step-cardinality-bound; hence the total is 0+4+3=7.","statement":"The exhaustive count is seven."}],"question_status":"answered","tool_requests":[]}}},"objective":"investigate","output_schema":{"additionalProperties":false,"properties":{"answer":{"maxLength":12000,"type":"string"},"approaches":{"items":{"additionalProperties":false,"properties":{"description":{"maxLength":4