{"answer":"There are exactly 7 subsets:\n\\[{1,8},\\ {2,7},\\ {3,6},\\ {4,5},\\ {1,2,6},\\ {1,3,5},\\ {2,3,4}.\\]\nFor example, \\({1,8}\\) has sum 9. Exhaustiveness follows by cardinality: no subset of four or more distinct positive elements can sum to 9, since its sum is at least \\(1+2+3+4=10\\); the remaining sizes are completely enumerated below.","approaches":[{"description":"Classify qualifying subsets by cardinality, exclude cardinalities at least four using the minimum possible sum, and enumerate pairs and triples.","id":"cardinality-casework","outcome":"candidate","reason":"It gives a disjoint, exhaustive partition and directly verifies all possible remaining cases."},{"description":"Partition qualifying subsets by their unique largest element and enumerate the residual sum from smaller elements.","id":"max-element-partition","outcome":"candidate","reason":"This is also valid: its cases m=4,5,6,7,8 yield the same seven subsets. The cardinality route is retained because its cutoff excludes every larger subset in one step."},{"description":"Inspect every one of the 256 subsets directly.","id":"brute-force-enumeration","outcome":"rejected","reason":"It is unnecessary and gives no structural explanation of exhaustiveness."}],"change_log":[],"claims":[{"basis":"question_premise","basis_reference":"Question premise: \"subsets of {1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8} have sum 9\"","citations":[],"critical":false,"depends_on":[],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"problem-definition","kind":"definition","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"The objects counted are subsets of {1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8} whose element-sum is 9.","step_ids":[],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Derived in cardinality-bound.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":["problem-definition"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"cardinality-bound-claim","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"Every qualifying subset has at most three elements.","step_ids":["cardinality-bound"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Derived in small-cardinality-cases.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":["problem-definition","cardinality-bound-claim"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"complete-list-claim","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"The qualifying subsets are exactly {1,8}, {2,7}, {3,6}, {4,5}, {1,2,6}, {1,3,5}, and {2,3,4}.","step_ids":["small-cardinality-cases"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Derived by the disjoint cardinality partition in total-count.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":["cardinality-bound-claim","complete-list-claim"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"count-claim","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"Exactly 7 subsets have sum 9.","step_ids":["total-count"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Established in small-cardinality-cases.","citations":[],"critical":false,"depends_on":["complete-list-claim"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"example-claim","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"{1,8} is a qualifying subset.","step_ids":["small-cardinality-cases"],"tool_ids":[]}],"open_questions":[],"proof_steps":[{"citations":[],"depends_on":[],"id":"cardinality-bound","justification":"A subset with at least four distinct positive elements has sum at least 1+2+3+4=10. Therefore it cannot have sum 9.","statement":"A qualifying subset has at most three elements."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["cardinality-bound"],"id":"small-cardinality-cases","justification":"The empty set sums to 0, and no singleton qualifies because every available element is at most 8. For a pair {a,b} with a<b and a+b=9, a=1,2,3,4, giving {1,8}, {2,7}, {3,6}, {4,5}. For a triple a<b<c with a+b+c=9: if a=1, then b+c=8 and b=2 or 3, giving {1,2,6} and {1,3,5}; if a=2, then b+c=7 and b=3, giving {2,3,4}; if a>=3, then a+b+c>=3+4+5=12. Thus these are all qualifying subsets of sizes at most three.","statement":"There are no qualifying subsets of size zero or one, exactly four qualifying pairs, and exactly three qualifying triples."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["cardinality-bound","small-cardinality-cases"],"id":"total-count","justification":"Cardinality classes are disjoint and exhaustive. The classes of sizes zero and one contribute 0, size two contributes 4, size three contributes 3, and larger sizes are excluded by cardinality-bound. Hence the total is 0+4+3=7.","statement":"The exhaustive count is 7."}],"question_status":"answered","tool_requests":[]}
